<blockquote>
<p>is the psychological improvement anomalous?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, probably should have stated that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What is the underlying narrative here, if any? If there isn’t one, how do you plan to maintain the reader’s attention through unconnected anecdotes?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is where I found myself a bit stuck I must admit. Partly the reason for desiring to have all personnel enter as a group was so that I could allow the characters to build off of each other. In fact, I think it will serve to make the narrative better to have the characters enter as a group. I want to tell a story about the effects of a single action, how one action can cause a domino effect. And also a story about a group of characters coming to terms with their trauma. Perhaps changing it so you do not *need* to kill the manifestations will make the characters psychological improvements seem more genuine? Perhaps even raise the question to if it is an anomalous property? Instead, the characters need to overcome flaws such as a cowardly D-Class finally standing up for themselves and the group causes their manifested trauma weaker or appear less.</p>

